.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt LINK 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm link ,
.Nm linkat ,
.Nm unlink ,
.Nm unlinkat
.Nd create a hard link or remove a file name
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn link "const char *oldpath" "const char *newpath"
.Ft int
.Fn linkat "int olddirfd" "const char *oldpath" "int newdirfd" "const char *newpath" "int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn unlink "const char *path"
.Ft int
.Fn unlinkat "int dirfd" "const char *path" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn link
creates
.Fa newpath
as another name for the file at
.Fa oldpath .
Hard links are implemented only on ext2, within one mount, and cannot
refer to directories.
The destination must not already exist.
Both paths and the source are checked before the kernel reports that
the file system does not support hard links.
The final symbolic link in
.Fa oldpath
is not followed.
An
.Fa oldpath
that ends in a slash, or whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
names a directory, as on Linux: its final link is followed, and it
returns
.Er ENOTDIR
unless it reaches a directory, which is
.Er EPERM .
A
.Fa newpath
that names a directory by its end makes nothing: with a slash after its
last name it returns
.Er ENOENT
when nothing has that name and
.Er EEXIST
when something does, and with a last component of
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
it returns
.Er EEXIST ,
as the root does, before
.Er EXDEV
and the refusal of a file system without hard links.
A
.Fa newpath
that exists returns
.Er EEXIST
before
.Er EXDEV ,
the refusals of a directory source and of a file system without hard
links, and
.Er EROFS ,
as on Linux.
.Pp
.Fn link
and
.Fn unlink
resolve relative paths from the current working directory.
.Fn linkat
resolves a relative
.Fa oldpath
from
.Fa olddirfd
and a relative
.Fa newpath
from
.Fa newdirfd .
Each directory argument may be
.Dv AT_FDCWD
to use the current working directory.
An absolute path ignores its directory argument.
Directory descriptors are resolved to paths; they do not bypass search
permission on ancestor directories.
.Pp
For
.Fn linkat ,
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW
in
.Fa flags
follows the source's final symbolic link.
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
is accepted as a flag but has no effect: an empty path still returns
.Er ENOENT .
Other flag bits return
.Er EINVAL .
These constants are declared in
.In fcntl.h .
.Pp
.Fn unlink
removes
.Fa path
without following its final symbolic link.
On KRFFS, the boot root and a mounted volume alike, a file that a
descriptor refers to, an
.Dv O_PATH
one included, becomes nameless and remains accessible through that
descriptor until the last close.
It is absent from path lookup and directory listings.
Without a descriptor, its storage is freed at unlink.
A file on the boot root which backs an attached loop device instead
returns
.Er EBUSY .
On ext2, the loop device retains the inode and the file may be unlinked.
.Pp
.Fn unlinkat
resolves a relative
.Fa path
from the directory
.Fa dirfd
is open on, or from the current working directory for
.Dv AT_FDCWD ,
and removes it as
.Fn unlink
does; with
.Dv AT_REMOVEDIR
in
.Fa flags
it removes an empty directory as
.Xr rmdir 2
does.
Any other flag returns
.Er EINVAL
before the path is read.
.Pp
Path traversal requires search permission on each ancestor directory.
Creating a link requires write permission on the destination directory;
unlinking requires write permission on the containing directory.
In a sticky directory, unlink also requires effective user ID 0,
ownership of the directory, or ownership of the entry.
Write permission on the entry alone does not satisfy the sticky rule.
The directory write check precedes the sticky check.
The KRFFS boot root is treated as mode 0755 and non-sticky.
.Pp
A directory is removed with
.Xr rmdir 2 .
.Fn unlink
returns
.Er EISDIR
for the root or a directory on the KRFFS boot root, ext2, FAT32, or exFAT,
and for a path whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
which names a directory.
A path with a slash after its last name names one too: a directory
there returns
.Er EISDIR ,
a name nothing has
.Er ENOENT ,
and anything else, a symbolic link to a directory among them,
.Er ENOTDIR ,
as on Linux, and nothing is removed.
On the KRFFS boot root the directory's write permission is checked
first.
A mounted KRFFS volume reports
.Er EIO
for its directory refusal.
A mount point is busy, and the synthetic
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc
namespaces are read-only.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 9 for
.Nm link ,
303 for
.Nm linkat ,
10 for
.Nm unlink ,
and 301 for
.Nm unlinkat .
On x86-64 they are 86, 265, 87 and 263.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success, or -1 on failure and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/fdstale.c
opens a file, unlinks its name, and reads the original contents through
the descriptor.
It checks that seeking to the end returns the file's size.
Opening the removed name returns
.Er ENOENT
before and after the last close.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
Search permission was denied on an ancestor directory, or write
permission was denied on the directory being changed.
.It Bq Er EBADF
A relative
.Fn linkat
or
.Fn unlinkat
path uses an invalid or stale directory descriptor.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Path resolution entered a mount being mounted or unmounted,
.Fn unlink
selected a mount point,
.Fn unlinkat
with
.Dv AT_REMOVEDIR
selected the root, or the file on the KRFFS boot root backs an attached
loop device.
.It Bq Er EEXIST
The ext2 destination name already exists, or a
.Fn link
destination names one by its end or is the root.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A path cannot be read from user memory, or there is no current process.
.It Bq Er EFBIG
An ext2 directory cannot grow further or a required directory block
exceeds the file system's addressing limit.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn linkat
or
.Fn unlinkat
was given unsupported flags,
.Fn unlinkat
with
.Dv AT_REMOVEDIR
was given a path whose last component is
.Pa \&. ,
or the file system cannot supply required attributes.
.It Bq Er EIO
File system metadata could not be read or written, or the device was removed.
A mounted KRFFS volume also returns this error when unlinking a missing
entry or a directory.
.It Bq Er EISDIR
.Fn unlink
selected the root or a directory on the KRFFS boot root, ext2, FAT32,
or exFAT, or a path whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. .
.It Bq Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Bq Er EMLINK
The ext2 source already has 65000 links.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
A path, directory prefix, component, or symbolic-link expansion is too long.
The copied path and its directory prefix must fit 4096 bytes, including
the terminating null byte.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
A path is empty, the source or a required path component does not exist,
a
.Fn link
destination with a slash after its last name names nothing, or the
entry to unlink does not exist on a file system that reports this error.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The file system could not allocate storage needed to resolve a path.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
There is no space available to extend the ext2 destination directory.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A component used as a directory is not a directory, a relative
.Fn linkat
or
.Fn unlinkat
path uses a descriptor that is not a directory, a path that ends in a
slash, or a
.Fn link
source that ends in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
names something else, or
.Fn unlinkat
with
.Dv AT_REMOVEDIR
selected a nondirectory on a file system that reports this error.
.It Bq Er ENOTEMPTY
.Fn unlinkat
with
.Dv AT_REMOVEDIR
selected a directory that is not empty, or a path whose last component
is
.Pa \&.. .
.It Bq Er EPERM
A hard link was requested to a directory, to a synthetic object, or on
a writable file system without hard links, the sticky-directory rule
forbids removal, the file is immutable or append-only, or the directory
of the new name is immutable.
The KRFFS boot root refuses hard links before checking read-only state.
.It Bq Er EROFS
The operation requires a write on a read-only mount or in a synthetic
namespace, or the file system has no unlink operation.
.It Bq Er EXDEV
The hard-link source and destination are on different mounts.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chmod 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr rename 2 ,
.Xr rmdir 2 ,
.Xr symlink 2 ,
.Xr loop 4
